<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/expert/critique-and-art-discourse">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Critique is the practice\'s quality loop: <strong>a learnable grammar for seeing and saying
    what works</strong> — and discourse is the ecosystem every artist publishes into. The expert
    tier is fluency in both: making the work and navigating the conversation around it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Feldman\'s order, the contract, receiving, the ecosystem" widget="equationStep" config={critCfg} />

  <h2>The professional vocabulary</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Talking about your work:</strong> the artist statement is the marketing lesson\'s positioning (what, how, why — concrete over mystical); a portfolio read-aloud beats any statement (the work carries the argument when the fundamentals lessons did their job).</li>
    <li><strong>Group crits are the art school\'s engine:</strong> weekly, all-work-on-the-wall — the practicum where vocabulary meets nerve; the habit transfers to every collaborative critique culture (design studios, writers\' rooms — the film lesson\'s table reads).</li>
    <li><strong>Juried shows and portfolios:</strong> the selection process (slides now, jury panels) — entry is a numbers game (the statistics lesson\'s variance: acceptance rates are low; volume and fit beat perfection).</li>
    <li><strong>Where the ladder ends:</strong> critique is the discipline of seeing honestly — the visual-arts loop closes: perception lesson → fundamentals → craft → voice → discourse, each feeding back into the first. That loop is the subject.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Feldman, Varieties of Visual Experience; Sontag, On Photography; the literature lesson\'s intentional fallacy; the design lesson\'s review culture.</p>
</TopicPage>
